          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The Reissner-Nordström solution to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Maxwell+theory"} Einstein-Maxwell theory
            describes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            outside of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+charge"} electrically charged
            (and/or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetically charged
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            %em charged
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+hole"} black hole
          %h2#details Details
          %h3#metric Metric
          %p
            For
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+charge"} electric charge
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} pseudo-Riemannian metric
            for the Reissner-Nordström spacetime is, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polar+coordinates"} polar coordinates
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo ,
                  %mi r
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (t,r,\phi,\theta)
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 4
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo =
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4 - \{0\} = \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}_{\gt 0} \times S^2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ds
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Q
                      %mo ,
                      %mi P
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi H
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi r
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi r
                    %mn 2
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ds
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 2
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  ds^2_{M,Q,P} \coloneqq - H d t^2 + H^{-1} d r^2 + r^2 ds_{S^2}^2
          %p where (in natural units)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo ≔
                  %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %mfrac
                    %mi M
                    %mi r
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Q
                        %mn 2
                      %mo +
                      %msup
                        %mi P
                        %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi r
                        %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H \coloneqq 1 - \frac{M}{r} + \frac{Q^2 + P^2}{r^2}
                  \\,.
          %p
            The corresponding
            = succeed "/electromagnetic" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Faraday+tensor"} Faraday tensor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature 2-form
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mi Q
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi r
                        %mn 2
                  %mi d
                  %mi t
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %mi r
                  %mo +
                  %mi P
                  %mi sin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi d
                  %mi θ
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F = \frac{Q}{r^2} d t \wedge d r + P \sin(\theta) d\theta \wedge d\phi
                  \\,.
          %blockquote
            %p (check sign)
          %h3#horizons Horizons
          %p
            The function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            above vanishes at
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mo ±
                  %mo ≔
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ±
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi M
                          %mn 2
                        %mo −
                        %mn 4
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi P
                          %mn 2
                        %mo +
                        %msup
                          %mi Q
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  r_{\pm}
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\tfrac{1}{2}\left(
                  M \pm \sqrt{M^2 - 4 (P^2 + Q^2)}
                  \\right)
                  \\,.
          %p
            The larger of the two solutions is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/event+horizon"} event horizon
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+horizon"} Cauchy horizon
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Q
                          %mn 2
                        %mo +
                        %msup
                          %mi P
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M = 2\sqrt{Q^2 + P^2}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extremal+black+hole"} extremal black hole
            (at least when also
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P = 0
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#PenroseDiagrams Penrose diagrams
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Penrose+diagram"} Penrose diagram
            for maximally extended RN black holes (from
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#HE73"} HE 73, Section 5.5
          %p
            %br/
          %div{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/RNBHPenroseDiagramHEFig25.jpg", :width => "550px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                From
                %a{:href => "#HE73"} HE 73, Fig. 25
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %br/
          %div{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/RNBHPenroseDiagramHEFig26.jpg", :width => "550px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                From
                %a{:href => "#HE73"} HE 73, Fig. 26
          %h3#InteriorSolutions Interior solutions
          %p Instead of having a mass and charge of singular support at the singular locus of the RN-spacetime, one may consider a finite mass and charge distribution, i.e. a “charged star”. Outside of this mass/charge distribution then the metric is the RN-spacetime as above, but in the interior it becomes smoothed out to a non-singular interior solution.
          %p
            Several attempts have been made to round out the Reissner metric with an interior.
            %a{:href => "#TiwariRaoKanakamelda85"} Tiwari-Rao-Kanakamedala 85
            found an interior with the condition
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 11
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 44
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{11} g_{44} = 1
            %a{:href => "#KyleMartin67"} Kyle-Martin 67
            found a rather complicated solution. They discussed the self-energy of the fields of charged matter.
            %a{:href => "#Wilson69"} Wilson 69
            modified this solution assuming a different value for the total charge.
            %a{:href => "#CohenCohen69"} Cohen-Cohen 69
            applied this solution to the special case of a charged thin shell and showed that the energy density of the electromagnetic field contributes to the mass.
            %a{:href => "#Boulware73"} Boulware 73
            studied the time development of thin shells.
            %a{:href => "#GravesBrill60"} Graves-Brill 60
            considered a possible oscillatory character of the Reissner-Nordström metric by examining the metric by Kruskal-like coordinates
            %a{:href => "#Bekenstein71"} Bekenstein 71
            studied another ansatz for the stress-energy-tensor of charged matter.
            %a{:href => "#KroriJayantimala75"} Krori-Jayantimala 75
            made use of the same ansatz, and he found a solution free of singularities.
            %a{:href => "#GautreauHoffman73"} Gautreau-Hoffman 73
            studied the sources of Weyl-type electrovac fields. They obtained the parameters for the source with the junction condition for the exterior solution.
            %a{:href => "#Efinger64"} Efinger 64
            deduced the stability of a charged particle from the self-energy of the gravitation field. In
            %a{:href => "#Burghardt09"} Burghardt 09
            is constructed another interior solution with the help of embedding the geometry in a 5-dimensional flat space.
          %p
            (Literature summary taken from
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Burghardt09"} Burghardt 09
          %p
            See also (
            %a{:href => "#Mehra82"}> Mehra 82
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#WX87"} WX 87
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#GuptaKumarPratibha12"} Gupta-Kumar-Pratibha 12
